<p>There has been a lot of chatter in the papers recently (well, several years) about trans women and whether they should be allowed to compete in women's sports.</p>
<p>Like many problems, this one is both definitional and fundamental.  I'm not going to really go there, but I asked myself what the next layer of the onion looks like.  Why are there women's sports?</p>
<p>There are, for example, age categories of sports (under 21, under 16).  These obviously exist because people at those ages are smaller, less physically developed and less experienced.</p>
<p>There is a competition for  <a href='https://www.nzrugby.co.nz/teams-and-competitions/national/under-85kg-club-cup'>men under 85kgs</a> to play rugby in New Zealand.  This enables lighter, but skillful and coordinated men to play other, similar men without excessive risk of physical harm.</p>
<p>And there are many levels of sports leagues based on overall ability and physical condition.  At lower levels, many of these offer mixed (i.e. non-segregated men and women) participation.  Women can compete in these at their own level of fitness and competence.  Why, then, do women need their own leagues?</p>
<p>It seems that the simple and obvious answer is that while a "mid-range" woman may be at a level around about a "mid-range" man, and certainly a lot fitter than me, for elite women, they are going to find themselves surrounded by "above-average" but certainly not elite men, most of whom will still be able to physically dominate them and will not need to train as hard in order to compete.  In other words, because of the physical disparities between men and women, the highest level a woman can aspire to will be mainly occupied by men who could achieve more if they tried and men who have a lot of physical strength but lack skill.  Why would a woman want to aspire to that?</p>
<p>It therefore makes sense that elite women athletes would want to withdraw from that situation and find other, like-minded, elite women athletes with whom to share their sporting ambitions.  And that would involve them forming teams from which such men were excluded.  And, given that sex is such an obvious dividing line in our society, excluding <i>all</i> men seems the obvious step.</p>
<p>I would then grant that if such a league felt the desire to include any specific men into their league - presumably because they fit the physical and emotional profile of the other members of the league - that would be their prerogative.  But it seems wrong that they should be forced to accept into their league the very types of people they were trying to "run away from" - just because those individuals see themselves as "women" in some sense.</p>
<p>I am definitely not an expert on the human phenotype.  But if I were, I would propose renaming women's sports as something very precise that describes the kind of people that are welcome in the league based on their physical characteristics and the required level of training to reach a certain level of physical fitness.</p>
<p>I am sure that most women playing women's sports appreciate being separate from men <i>qua</i> men to begin with, but I do not think that this is at all germane to the discussion here.  It is one of physical characteristics and what the human body is capable of.  And there is a huge divide between what elite women are capable of and what genetic men are capable of.  And that should not be trivialized in a search for "equality" where none is to be found.</p>
